 Small operations repository for the log retention job.
 
-`bin/archive-logs.sh SERVICE` packs the logs of one service from `logs/` into a dated
-tarball under `archives/`. It runs from cron on the log host every night.
+## Usage
 
-Archives are pruned by hand today.
+```
+bin/archive-logs.sh [--keep-days N] SERVICE [SERVICE...]
+```
+
+Packs the logs of each named service from `logs/SERVICE` into a dated tarball,
+`archives/SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz`. Paths are relative to the current directory, so run it
+from the repository root. It runs from cron on the log host every night.
+
+- Several services can be given in one run: `bin/archive-logs.sh api web`.
+- `--keep-days N` (or `--keep-days=N`) removes archives older than N days from `archives/`
+  after the new archives are in place. Only files named `SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz` directly
+  inside `archives/` are considered. Without the option nothing is removed.
+- Service names must match `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*` (at most 64 characters) and have a
+  directory under `logs/`. All names are checked before anything is archived, and the new
+  archives are only published if every one of them was built, so a bad name or a failed
+  `tar` leaves `archives/` unchanged and prunes nothing.
+- On success it prints one summary line, for example:
+  `archived 2 service(s): api web; pruned 3 archive(s) older than 14 day(s)`
+- Exit status: 0 on success, 64 for bad usage, 65 for an invalid service name, 66 for a
+  missing log or archive directory; any other non-zero status is a failed command.
+
+Requires Bash 4.4 or later.
